<p>Norwegian police are investigating an overnight explosion outside the U.S. Embassy in Oslo. Authorities said the blast caused only minor damage and no injuries, and reporting from the area described a swift security response. Police said the incident appears to have been a targeted attack. They are looking at a possible terrorism motive among other lines of inquiry, and stressed that other motives are still on the table as the investigation continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/4AF_-zYVzmEF-MqXDXR-NZx5I57_o5_5SPDMQuxTuD770LSaM3HWPIC5tUz6dkkcLsXIjkn7G_LS_LtNXPtjtxlrvcgollaePYKMRXMVDuLVPd_h4TrfIjkBoMT_w4NiqT6470K4PusjNiyPAaou3G4yewhWH0DG4tnxwyTVb7AwkJYlp-2NyPo6' alt='Norwegian police and technicians at the entrance of the United States Embassy in Oslo after a loud bang was reported on Sunday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Initial reports: Residents told police they heard a “loud bang” overnight, prompting officers to head to the area near the embassy.</li><li>Lines of inquiry: Investigators said a terrorism motive is one possibility, but they are also pursuing other explanations.</li><li>On-scene response: Reporting described police cordoning off the area and bringing in specialists to examine the embassy entrance after the explosion.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Oslo police: Police said the blast appears to have been a targeted attack, and they sent officers and technicians to examine the area around the embassy entrance as the investigation got underway. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/08/world/europe/explosion-us-embassy-oslo.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Norwegian authorities (investigators): Investigators said a terrorism motive is one possibility they are examining, while emphasizing that they have not ruled out other motives. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yjegg892lo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>U.S. Embassy area reporting: Coverage highlighted the quick security response and the police cordon around th